The experimental B3 backend normalizes native integer division and modulo, including eligible typed bodies and active universal definitions. It adds native Real types, exact bounded rational literals, real arithmetic and division, integer embedding, floor, and eligible actual prelude conversion definitions. Existing nonzero-divisor and fractional-conversion obligations are preserved; no arithmetic assumptions are added. Fresh Real library verification and focused acceptance remain pending. The checked B3 language additionally has bounded positive-width native bitvector sorts, canonical literals, typed primitive operations, and aggregate resource admission. The normalizer translates bounded positive-width native bitvector literals, bitwise and modular arithmetic, unsigned comparisons and division, same-width shifts, extract/concat, exact native conversions, and eligible actual typed Body substitutions. BV0 keeps the source Int alias and literal bodies. Source guards remain explicit. Fresh bitvector library, worker and normalization acceptance and power remain pending. Exact source-owned unsigned conversion wrappers now have a narrow direct raw-command route with independent per-context availability certificates and an exhaustive substitution validator. Uncaptured specification, call, where and synthesized roots and actual Body chains fail closed; no unsigned equality or range axiom is added. The new source controls and corpus expectations are unexecuted. Positive-width rotations now lower to portable remainder/subtraction/shifts/OR through three fresh nested lets, preserving the original count and conversion checks. Their English bit-index correspondence and strict native-discrepancy/anti-vacuity controls remain pending execution; default Boogie behavior is unchanged.
 Worker runtime extraction checks now require the resolver's exact synthetic return-label wrapper before extracting the unchanged single check.
